\lispPackageChapter{yahoo-finance/rest-api}

The thin \textsc{Http} layer.  It sends a browser \texttt{User-Agent},
retries 429s, parses \textsc{Json} with Yason (arrays as vectors), and
exposes one wrapper for Yahoo's unofficial chart endpoint.

\section{Variables}

\lispVariableSubsection{*query2*}

\url{https://query2.finance.yahoo.com} --- the default host.  Query1
429s more readily in practice.

\lispVariableSubsection{*query1*}

\url{https://query1.finance.yahoo.com}.  Set \texttt{*api*} to this if
query2 is unhappy.

\lispVariableSubsection{*api*}

The host actually used for requests.  Defaults to \texttt{*query2*}.

\lispVariableSubsection{*user-agent*}

A Chrome-shaped \texttt{User-Agent} string.  Yahoo returns 429 for
requests that look like a bare \textsc{Http} library.

\lispVariableSubsection{*api-cache-timeout-seconds*}

How long \texttt{http-get-cached} and \texttt{http-get-json-cached} keep
a response.  Default 60.

\lispVariableSubsection{*max-retries*}

How many times to retry a 429.  Default 3.

\lispVariableSubsection{*retry-wait-seconds*}

Base wait, in seconds, between 429 retries.  Attempt $n$ waits
$n \times$ this value.  Default 2.

\section{Functions}

\lispFunctionSubsection{canonicalize-ticker}

Yahoo tickers are uppercase strings: \texttt{AAPL}, \texttt{BRK-B},
\texttt{\textasciicircum GSPC}.  Symbols are upcased too.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(canonicalize-ticker \lispVar{symbol})}

\lispFunctionSubsection{get-chart}

Raw \texttt{GET /v8/finance/chart/\lispVar{symbol}}.  Returns the
top-level Yason hash table.  This is the thin wrapper: it does
\emph{not} rewrite \texttt{range=max}.  Use \texttt{ohlc-history} for a
Lisp series that avoids Yahoo's quarterly downsample.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(get-chart \lispVar{symbol} \&key \lispVar{interval} \lispVar{range}
\lispVar{period1} \lispVar{period2} \lispVar{events}
\lispVar{include-adjusted-close} \lispVar{include-pre-post} \lispVar{cache})}

\texttt{include-adjusted-close} defaults to true.  \texttt{cache}
defaults to true.  \texttt{nil} query values are omitted.

\lispFunctionSubsection{chart-path}

The request path, with the ticker \textsc{Url}-encoded so
\texttt{\textasciicircum GSPC} works.

\lispFunctionSubsection{chart-uri}

The full \textsc{Url} \texttt{get-chart} would request.  Useful as a
candlesticks retrieval endpoint.

\lispFunctionSubsection{unwrap-chart}

The first \texttt{chart.result} element of a payload, or a
\texttt{chart-error} if Yahoo set \texttt{chart.error} or the result is
empty.  Yahoo sometimes returns \textsc{Http} 200 with an error object
(a delisted symbol).

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(unwrap-chart \lispVar{payload} \&key \lispVar{url})}

\section{Conditions}

\lispTypeSubsection{api-error}

An error talking to Yahoo.  Accessors:
\texttt{api-error-status}, \texttt{api-error-url},
\texttt{api-error-code}, \texttt{api-error-message},
\texttt{api-error-timestamp}, \texttt{api-error-body}.

\lispTypeSubsection{rate-limited}

\textsc{Http} 429.  Subclass of \texttt{api-error}.  \texttt{http-get}
retries these with backoff before signalling.

\lispTypeSubsection{chart-error}

The chart payload carried an error object, or had no result.  Accessors
\texttt{chart-error-code} and \texttt{chart-error-description} hold
Yahoo's \texttt{code} / \texttt{description} fields.

The package also exports the shared \textsc{Http} helpers
(\texttt{http-get}, \texttt{http-get-json}, \texttt{api-get}, query
builders, \texttt{json-ref}) in the same style as
\texttt{coingecko/rest-api}.
